@node Main "RadeonGL"

@{b}RadeonGL 0.9@{ub} -- minigl.library for the ATI Radeon 8500 to 9250

RadeonGL is a minigl.library that draws with the 3D engine of an ATI
Radeon from the R200 family, on a graphics card that Picasso96 drives. It
needs no Warp3D: it talks to the Radeon's own command processor, through
Picasso96, while the Workbench keeps running.

    @{" What RadeonGL is                     " link About}
    @{" What you need                        " link Requirements}
    @{" Installing, and going back           " link Install}
    @{" Testing the card                     " link Test}
    @{" The demos                            " link Demos}
    @{" Which programs work                  " link Programs}
    @{" The screen                           " link Screen}
    @{" Settings                             " link Settings}
    @{" Speed                                " link Speed}
    @{" The log file                         " link Log}
    @{" When something goes wrong            " link Trouble}
    @{" Limits of this version               " link Limits}
    @{" How it works                         " link Inside}
    @{" Licence, credits and AI              " link Licences}
    @{" History                              " link History}
@endnode

@node About "What RadeonGL is"
@toc Main
@next Requirements

@{b}What RadeonGL is@{ub}

Programs such as GLQuake draw through minigl.library, the Amiga's small
OpenGL library. RadeonGL is a minigl.library of its own: the same
library name, the same programming interface (minigl.library 29.1,
dispatch ABI 5), but behind it the Radeon's 3D hardware instead of
Warp3D or a software renderer.

@{b}What it does@{ub}

    * draws textured, coloured and blended triangles with the Radeon's
      own transformation and rasterising hardware, and lets it light
      them, fog them and compute texture coordinates
    * opens a Picasso96 screen of its own for the program, in 16 or 32
      bit, or draws in a window on the Workbench, or into the program's
      own bitmap
    * lets several programs draw at once
    * keeps textures in the card's memory
    * checks every list of commands before the Radeon runs it, and
      stops a faulty one before it reaches the card (this can be turned
      off for speed)
    * leaves the Workbench and every other program running

RadeonGL is new. It has been written and tested with GLQuake on one
machine (see @{" Speed " link Speed}). Other programs may need functions
it does not have yet: see @{" Which programs work " link Programs}.
@endnode

@node Requirements "What you need"
@toc Main
@prev About
@next Install

@{b}What you need@{ub}

    * @{b}A Radeon of the R200 family@{ub}: Radeon 8500, 9000, 9100, 9200
      or 9250 (the chips R200, RV250 and RV280). Other Radeons are refused.
    * @{b}Picasso96 3.6 or newer@{ub}, with its Radeon driver
      (LIBS:Picasso96/Radeon.card) driving that card. RadeonGL finds the
      card through Picasso96 and nowhere else.
    * @{b}A 68040 or 68060 with FPU@{ub}, or @{b}a 68020 or 68030 with
      a 68881 or 68882@{ub}. RadeonGL comes in two builds, one for each;
      the Installer picks the right one. The 68040/68060 build is made
      for the 68060; on a 68040 it needs the 68040.library as usual. A
      build on a CPU it cannot run on does not open at all.
    * @{b}AmigaOS 3.1 or newer@{ub}. It has been tested on AmigaOS 3.2.
    * @{b}Screen modes@{ub} on the Radeon in the sizes your programs use,
      for example 320 x 240 or 640 x 480, made with the Picasso96Mode
      preferences: see @{" The screen " link Screen}.
    * @{b}Free card memory@{ub} for RadeonGL's own buffers and at least
      4 MB of textures. It takes up to 16 MB for textures when the card
      has it.

@{b}Tested on@{ub}

An A4000D with a CyberStorm MK II 68060 at 50 MHz, a Mediator PCI
bridge and a Radeon 9200, under AmigaOS 3.2 and Picasso96.

Reported by a tester: an A4000D with a BFG9060 68060 at 75 MHz and a
Prometheus Firestorm PCI bridge. RadeonGL works with both bridges.
@endnode

@node Install "Installing, and going back"
@toc Main
@prev Requirements
@next Test

@{b}Installing@{ub}

Double-click @{b}Install@{ub} in the RadeonGL drawer. The Installer

    * checks the CPU, the FPU and Picasso96,
    * picks the build for your CPU (it asks, from the Intermediate user
      level on), and puts that minigl.library into LIBS:,
    * keeps a minigl.library that was there before and is not RadeonGL's
      as LIBS:minigl.library.old (or .old1, when there is an .old
      already),
    * makes a RadeonGL drawer with these manuals, the card test, the card
      report, the demos and the licences, where you choose (Work: by
      default),
    * asks whether the command check stays on (see @{" Settings " link Settings});
      at the Novice level it keeps what is set.

@{b}By hand@{ub}

Copy Files/Libs/minigl.library (68040/68060) or
Files/68030/Libs/minigl.library (68020/68030 with an FPU) from the
package into LIBS:, after renaming the old one. Then, or reboot:

    Avail FLUSH

A library stays in memory until it is flushed, so without this the next
program would still meet the old one.

@{b}Going back@{ub}

Rename LIBS:minigl.library.old back to LIBS:minigl.library (delete
RadeonGL's first), then Avail FLUSH or reboot.
@endnode

@node Test "Testing the card"
@toc Main
@prev Install
@next Demos

@{b}Test@{ub}

Double-click @{b}Test@{ub} in the RadeonGL drawer. It opens a 640 x 480
screen on the Radeon and draws 300 frames: a spinning triangle that
crosses a red and white chequered square, and a small patch of the
square that changes colour every frame. Then it shows the frame rate
and what the library saw. Ctrl-C stops it early.

If you see a small window "RadeonGL null card" instead, RadeonGL did not
find a Radeon it can use: see @{" When something goes wrong " link Trouble}.

@{b}CardReport@{ub}

Double-click @{b}CardReport@{ub} for a report of the card as RadeonGL
sees it: the Picasso96 board, the chip, its memory and its main
registers. It reports the board the Workbench screen is on, and only
reads it. The report goes to RAM:RadeonGL-Report.txt; please add it to
any bug report.
@endnode

@node Demos "The demos"
@toc Main
@prev Test
@next Programs

@{b}The demos@{ub}

The Demos drawer holds four small programs that show the Radeon at work.
Double-click one; Esc ends it, and its window then shows the frame rate.

@{b}Spinner@{ub}
    A textured ring turns about three axes in front of a colour gradient,
    its colours lit at every corner by a light that stays where it is.

@{b}Glow@{ub}
    Sparks with comet tails circle inside a slowly turning crystal of
    tinted glass. The glass is see-through, the sparks add up their light.

@{b}Landscape@{ub}
    A flight low over rolling hills under a dusk sky, the ground drawn with
    two textures: grass and earth, and a light texture shaded from the
    slopes.

@{b}Showcase@{ub}
    The three in turn, twelve seconds each. The space bar skips to the
    next one.

From a Shell they take FRAMES (0: until Esc), WIDTH, HEIGHT and DEPTH
(16 or 32), for example:

    Spinner FRAMES=600 WIDTH=320 HEIGHT=240

On the test machine (640 x 480, 16 bit, command check off): Spinner 60,
Glow 36, Landscape 64 frames a second.
@endnode

@node Programs "Which programs work"
@toc Main
@prev Demos
@next Screen

@{b}Which programs work@{ub}

RadeonGL is a minigl.library 29.1 with the dispatch interface (ABI 5) of
the current MiniGL SDK. Programs built with that SDK open it like any
other minigl.library 29.

    * @{b}GLQuake@{ub} built for the shared minigl.library runs, all three
      demos, in 320 x 240 up to 1280 x 720.
    * @{b}Programs for older MiniGL versions@{ub} (minigl.library 1.x or
      2.x, built against Warp3D) use a different interface and do not
      run with RadeonGL.
    * @{b}Other programs@{ub} for minigl.library 29 may call functions
      RadeonGL does not have yet. Such a call draws nothing and sets the
      error GL_INVALID_OPERATION (glGetError); the @{" log file " link Log}
      lists them under "slots called but not implemented". Please report
      them.

Several programs can draw through RadeonGL at the same time, each on a
screen of its own, in a window or into a bitmap (see
@{" Limits of this version " link Limits}).
@endnode

@node Screen "The screen"
@toc Main
@prev Programs
@next Settings

@{b}The screen@{ub}

A program that asks minigl.library for a display gets a Picasso96 screen
of its own on the Radeon, in the size it asks for:

    * RadeonGL takes the Picasso96 mode that fits that size best, in
      16 bit (R5G6B5) if there is one, else in 32 bit.
    * The screen has to be on the Radeon. If the best mode is on another
      board, or there is none, RadeonGL cannot draw.
    * Make the modes your programs need with the Picasso96Mode
      preferences, for the Radeon board: GLQuake uses 320 x 240 or
      640 x 480 unless told otherwise, Test 640 x 480.
    * The largest screen is 2048 x 2048.
    * A program that lists the modes (GLQuake's Video Options) gets every
      mode of the Radeon board, and can switch to another size while it
      runs. If one does not fit in the card's memory, the program is told
      why.
    * With vertical sync on (GLQuake: on by default) each picture goes to
      the screen while the beam is outside it: no tearing when turning.

You can switch to the Workbench and back while a program draws: the
Workbench and the other programs keep running. While the GL screen is
behind another one, RadeonGL draws nothing; the program goes on.

@{b}In a window, or into a program's bitmap@{ub}

A program can also draw in a window, on the Workbench or another screen,
when that screen is on the Radeon and has 15, 16 or 32 bits (R5G5B5PC,
R5G6B5PC, B8G8R8A8):

    * in its own window (mglCreateContextFromWindow): RadeonGL draws each
      picture on the card and copies it into the window's inner area at
      mglSwitchDisplay;
    * in a window RadeonGL opens on the default public screen
      (mglChooseWindowMode before mglCreateContext);
    * into a bitmap of the program's on the Radeon
      (mglCreateContextFromBitMap): RadeonGL draws straight into it, and
      the program shows it, alone or together with its own drawing.

A 24-bit (R8G8B8, B8G8R8) or 8-bit screen takes no GL window: the Radeon
cannot draw in those formats. Such a program gets no requester; the
@{" log file " link Log} says why. A window or bitmap context starts with
8 MB of texture memory (RadeonGLHeapMax sets less).

Over DVI, modes with a pixel clock above about 110 MHz (1680 x 1050 and
larger at 60 Hz) showed dots or dropped out on the test machine while the
Radeon drew. Reduced-blanking timings in the Picasso96Mode preferences
lower the clock.
@endnode

@node Settings "Settings"
@toc Main
@prev Screen
@next Speed

@{b}Settings@{ub}

RadeonGL reads a few environment variables when a program opens its
display. Set one for this session with SetEnv, and for good with SetEnv
SAVE (AmigaOS 3.2) or by copying it from ENV: to ENVARC:. A variable
that is not set means the default.

@{b}RadeonGLCheck@{ub}  (default: on)
    0 turns the command check off. The check reads every list of commands
    before the Radeon runs it and stops one that would program anything a
    3D draw has no business with. It costs about a fifth of the frame
    rate. Off is faster, and safe as long as RadeonGL has no bug.

        SetEnv SAVE RadeonGLCheck 0     (off)
        UnSetEnv SAVE RadeonGLCheck     (on again)

@{b}RadeonGLLog@{ub}  (default: PROGDIR:radeongl.log)
    Where the @{" log file " link Log} goes. NIL: writes none.

        SetEnv SAVE RadeonGLLog NIL:

@{b}RadeonGLCard@{ub}  (default: the Radeon, else the null card)
    Radeon: draw on the Radeon or refuse, with a requester that says why.
    Null: never touch the card; programs run and show only a small
    window (for testing programs without a Radeon).

@{b}RadeonGLDirect@{ub}  (default: off)
    1 writes the vertices of each draw straight into the card's memory,
    with a short Picasso96 lock each time, instead of one lock a frame.
    About 2% faster on the test machine.

@{b}RadeonGLUploadCache@{ub}  (default: on)
    0 sends every vertex again in every frame (for comparing only).

@{b}RadeonGLLocked@{ub}  (default: on)
    0 draws a program's vertex arrays the slow, direct way (for
    comparing, or if indexed drawing misbehaves).

@{b}RadeonGLOverlap@{ub}  (default: on)
    0 makes every frame wait until the Radeon has drawn it before
    RadeonGL lets go of the card. On, the Radeon finishes a frame while
    the processor builds the next: GLQuake demo1 at 1280 x 720 went from
    19.9 to 23.5 fps on the test machine. Off is for comparing, or if
    pictures go wrong.

@{b}RadeonGLMove16@{ub}  (default: off)
    A 68060 only. 1 or 2 copies each frame's data into the card's memory
    in 16-byte bursts (MOVE16): 1 empties the data cache over it first,
    2 does not (enough on a 68060). Only for an accelerator and a bridge
    that pass bursts on: on the test machine (68060, Mediator) they
    arrive as single cycles and gain nothing. WARNING: on a board that
    cannot burst (the BFG9060, for one) the machine may hang. RadeonGL
    tries one line first and stays off if it comes back wrong; a hang it
    cannot catch.

@{b}RadeonGLDisplayPriority@{ub}  (default: not set)
    high lets the screen read the card's memory with high priority all
    the time while a program's display is open: against white pixels and
    a picture that goes black at large screen modes while the Radeon
    draws (the same as "DisplayPriority HIGH" in Linux' radeon driver).
    A number from 0 to 127 says instead how far the screen's buffer may
    run down before it gets priority (0 = always). Not set keeps the card
    driver's setting, which also comes back when the program quits.

        SetEnv SAVE RadeonGLDisplayPriority high
@endnode

@node Speed "Speed"
@toc Main
@prev Settings
@next Log

@{b}Speed@{ub}

GLQuake for R200 68k, the RadeonGL edition of GLQuake for
minigl.library (1.2, a separate archive): its timedemos (all frames, no
sound) on the test machine (A4000D, 68060 at 50 MHz on a CyberStorm MK
II, Mediator, Radeon 9200, AmigaOS 3.2, Picasso96 3.6), RadeonGL 0.8
with the command check off (RadeonGLCheck=0, as GLQuake's start window
sets it) and vertical sync off, frames a second:

    Demo   320 x 240   640 x 480   1280 x 720
    demo1       33.5        32.5         32.9
    demo2       30.6           -         26.0
    demo3       28.9           -         27.5

RadeonGL 0.9 runs GLQuake as fast as 0.8: demo1 at 320 x 240 took 29.1
seconds instead of 29.0, the smallest step GLQuake's timedemo shows.

Vertical sync (on in GLQuake by default) makes a frame wait for the
beam: about 2% in play at 800 x 600.

The same with the Aminet source built for the 68060, on RadeonGL 0.6:
demo1 24.4 / 23.7 / 23.3, demo2 21.7 / - / 17.9, demo3 21.4 / - / 19.9.
The Aminet binary itself is a 68020 build and slower still on a 68060.
With the command check on (RadeonGL as installed), every command list is
checked first: in 0.4 that took demo1 at 320 x 240 from 24.4 to 19.1.

A tester's A4000D (BFG9060, 68060 at 75 MHz, Prometheus Firestorm,
RadeonGL 0.6, check off, 8-bit LM, demo1 without sound): 43.0 frames a
second at 320 x 240, 41.5 at 640 x 480; direct 43.7 and 42.2. Its memory
is much faster than the test machine's: that, more than the clock, makes
the difference.

The build for the 68020/68030, on the same 68060 for comparison: demo1
17.1 (check on), 22.8 (check off), 23.7 (direct, 8-bit LM). A real 68030
is far slower than that; its figures will follow.

The Radeon is not what sets the pace: sixteen times the pixels cost
about one frame a second. The 68060 is: it runs the game, prepares every
draw for the card, and writes the commands and vertices into the card's
memory over the Zorro bus at about 9 MB a second.

So, to make a program faster:

    * turn the command check off (see @{" Settings " link Settings}),
    * close programs that use the CPU in the background, and screen
      blankers that wake up during a game,
    * use the program's own options that save CPU time (in GLQuake for
      example fewer particles or dynamic lights).
@endnode

@node Log "The log file"
@toc Main
@prev Speed
@next Trouble

@{b}The log file@{ub}

When a program closes its display, RadeonGL writes a short summary to
radeongl.log in the program's own drawer (or where
@{" RadeonGLLog " link Settings} says). It names the card and the screen
and counts the frames, the lock times, the texture memory used, and any
GL error or missing function.

If a program fails, that file says why more often than not. When the
Radeon cannot be used at all, the log holds the one line of the reason,
the same as the requester.

To have no log at all:

    SetEnv SAVE RadeonGLLog NIL:
@endnode

@node Trouble "When something goes wrong"
@toc Main
@prev Log
@next Limits

@{b}A small window "RadeonGL null card" and no picture@{ub}
    RadeonGL found no Radeon screen mode for the size the program wants,
    or no Radeon at all, and fell back to drawing nothing. Make the mode
    with Picasso96Mode (see @{" The screen " link Screen}). To get the
    reason instead: SetEnv RadeonGLCard Radeon, and run the program again.

@{b}A requester "The Radeon cannot be used"@{ub}
    The second line says why. The usual ones:

    "not an R200-family chip"
        The card is a Radeon, but not one of the 8500 to 9250.
    "no texture heap of 4 MB or more on the board"
        The card's memory is full: close screens on the Radeon (a big
        Workbench screen in 32 bit takes much of it) and try again.
    "did not put the ... bitmap on the board"
        The same: Picasso96 had no room in the card's memory.
    "the GL screen did not open"
        Picasso96 could not open the screen mode.
    "the aperture byte-swaps this screen's bitmaps"
        A screen format RadeonGL does not support yet.

@{b}"Unable to open minigl.library"@{ub}
    The program wants a different minigl.library version, or is built
    for the older Warp3D MiniGL (see @{" Which programs work " link Programs}).

@{b}The screen goes black after a while@{ub}
    A screen blanker or a monitor power saver (a DPMS commodity) can blank
    the Radeon's screen while a program draws. Quit them for the game.

@{b}White or missing textures@{ub}
    The texture memory is full (see @{" Limits " link Limits}); the log
    counts the refused textures.
@endnode

@node Limits "Limits of this version"
@toc Main
@prev Trouble
@next Inside

@{b}Limits of this version@{ub}

    * Several programs can draw at once, each on a screen of its own, in
      a window or into a bitmap (see @{" The screen " link Screen}). They
      take turns on the Radeon; while more than one draws, each is a
      little slower than alone. Each needs card memory of its own: a
      screen context up to 16 MB of textures, a window or bitmap context
      8 MB.
    * Lighting (glLight, glMaterial, glLightModel, glColorMaterial, normals
      from glNormal and glNormalPointer) is done by the Radeon: eight
      lights, directional, local and spot, with attenuation. Lit compiled
      vertex arrays go the fast way too, normals and all.
    * Texture coordinates can be generated by the Radeon (glTexGen:
      object linear, eye linear, sphere map; S and T), and texture
      matrices apply. Fog (glFog) and wide lines (glLineWidth) too.
    * glReadPixels and mglWriteShotPPM read the picture back from the
      card's memory through Picasso96: about 4 MB a second, so a
      640 x 480 picture in 32 bit takes about a quarter of a second.
    * Textures live in up to 16 MB of the card's memory, and RadeonGL
      does not yet swap them out: a program that needs more gets white
      textures for the rest.
    * Textures are stored with 16 bits a texel: RGBA textures lose
      precision (ARGB4444), RGB ones become RGB565 -- unless a program asks
      for GL_RGBA8 or GL_RGB8, which keep 8 bits a component (and twice
      the card's memory). GLQuake asks for neither.
    * Screens up to 2048 x 2048, in 16 or 32 bit.
    * The card is reached only through Picasso96 and its Radeon driver.

@{b}Not implemented yet@{ub}

RadeonGL implements 122 of the 205 functions of minigl.library 29.1:
everything GLQuake uses, and more. A program calling one of the others
gets nothing for it but the error GL_INVALID_OPERATION; the
@{" log file " link Log} names such calls.

    * Display lists (glNewList, glCallList and the rest).
    * glTexCoord4f, the texture environment GL_BLEND (drawn as
      GL_MODULATE) and GL_DECAL with an RGBA texture.
    * Stencil and accumulation buffers, line and polygon stipple, logic
      operations.
    * Reading the depth buffer, copying pixels (glCopyTexImage2D,
      glCopyTexSubImage2D), glDrawBuffer, glReadBuffer.
    * The double-precision calls (glLoadMatrixd, glMultMatrixd,
      glRotated, glScaled, glTranslated, glGetDoublev).
    * glArrayElement, glInterleavedArrays, glMultiDrawArrays, edge flags
      and colour-index mode.
    * glBlendEquation, glBlendFuncSeparate, glColorTable, glTexEnvfv,
      texture priorities and residency.
    * GLU and GLUT (gluPerspective, gluLookAt, gluBuild2DMipmaps, the
      quadrics; the GLUT window and shape functions).
    * MiniGL's own main loop and its callbacks (mglMainLoop, mglIdleFunc,
      mglKeyFunc, mglMouseFunc), mglLockBack, mglTexMemStat.
@endnode

@node Inside "How it works"
@toc Main
@prev Limits
@next Licences

@{b}How it works@{ub}

For each frame, RadeonGL builds the Radeon's commands in fast RAM: the
state changes, the vertices and the texture uploads, packed the way the
R200 reads them. At the end of the frame it locks its Picasso96 bitmaps
once, copies the commands into the card's memory, lets the Radeon's
command processor run them, waits until the frame is drawn, shows it,
and unlocks. In between, Picasso96 and the Workbench have the card as
always.

    * The command processor runs AMD's R200 microcode, loaded at the first
      use.
    * The texture memory, the command ring and the back buffer are
      Picasso96 bitmaps of their own: Picasso96 decides where they are,
      and RadeonGL asks again at every lock.
    * Vertices that a program sends again unchanged (GLQuake does that
      for most of its world and models) stay in the card's memory and are
      not sent twice.
    * The command check runs before the lock, on the commands in fast RAM.
@endnode

